{"title":"2024 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 25","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_Symmetry_+_Observations_=_5_min_solve\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 Symmetry + Observations = 5 min solve</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Pablo will decorate each of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> identical white balls with either a striped or a dotted pattern, using either red or blue paint. He will decide on the color and pattern for each ball by flipping a fair coin for each of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> decisions he must make. After the paint dries, he will place the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> balls in an urn. Frida will randomly select one ball from the urn and note its color and pattern. The events \"the ball Frida selects is red\" and \"the ball Frida selects is striped\" may or may not be independent, depending on the outcome of Pablo's coin flips. The probability that these two events are independent can be written as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/0/3d01476c4fc8b19ad9eb851ff22a83a669363212.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac mn,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"24\" height=\"33\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime positive integers. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/6/1863dad6a8c56abd7427d20e656ba8e5501e858c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m?$\" width=\"24\" height=\"12\" /> (Recall that two events <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are independent if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/91994d0e99f037449011c8a215fc2454faa232e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(A \\text{ and }B) = P(A) \\cdot P(B).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"224\" height=\"20\" />)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/3/9a36f8c9a6639d2e51c1263a2db40306e1adf025.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 243 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 245 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } 247 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 249\\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 251$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"446\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> be the number of balls that are both striped and red, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> be the number of balls that are striped but blue, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be the number of balls that are red but dotted. Then there must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/d/beda8984a02fe218cd3036409fffbf8cbdf066ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6-a-x-y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"107\" height=\"17\" /> balls that are dotted and blue.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be the event, \"the ball Frida selects is red\", and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> be the event, \"the ball Frida selects is striped\". <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are independent if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/e/b0e0de258a6595236a5a64c9a65333f5846c4944.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{a}{6}=\\frac{a+x}{6}\\cdot\\frac{a+y}{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"144\" height=\"37\" />, i.e.,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/0/46066340e6f269bf1a9ef907a08f4d2464abb67c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[6a=(a+x)(a+y)\\]\" width=\"153\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Before we continue, I'd like to clarify that the sample space <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, under which we are computing probability that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are independent, consists of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/d/85d9c5b07c9781abdf410d22ab3692498ae3dbdb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4^6$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" /> total outcomes, each equally likely. Notice that once given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/6/8f642ed2abe8685b3895be6e6726e2a582367030.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, x, y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"48\" height=\"13\" />, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/f/99fc787ca5f699b60be3a3b0dc03a5e9acdc278f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${6\\choose a}{6-a \\choose x}{6-a-x \\choose y}=\\frac{6!}{a!x!y!(6-a-x-y)!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"408\" height=\"55\" /> corresponding outcomes.\n</p><p>Now, it remains to find all nonnegative integer solutions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/7/e179334063fb5cc6e8e7980a92c3abb11e9031e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a, x, y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"62\" height=\"20\" /> to the above equation such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/f/5df0da4d920315497c528cd485424753a55fff43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+x+y&lt;6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"109\" height=\"17\" />, add up all the corresponding outcomes, and then divide by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/d/85d9c5b07c9781abdf410d22ab3692498ae3dbdb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4^6$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"15\" height=\"15\" />. We can find the solutions relatively easily by doing casework on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/b/54b449d861eec48f7fbd2e2af1dc42d92064933f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"148\" height=\"17\" />. Long story short, one finds the solutions to be\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/d/efd3787bdf4ab8f2d5619590a63fdab127f10fed.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(0, 0, 0), (0, 0, 1)\\cdots (0, 0, 6)\\]\" width=\"205\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/0/900ba38d22f8a73349555a2cd593be74e643adc1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(0, 1, 0), (0, 2, 0)\\cdots (0, 6, 0)\\]\" width=\"205\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/8/c9842833453baaeecb9fb305f771a60c4dc97b00.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(1, 5, 0), (1, 0, 5), (1, 2, 1), (1, 1, 2)\\]\" width=\"251\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/1/26106b2283214f4d3e63ee867666b322732310b2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(2, 4, 0), (2, 0, 4) (2, 2, 1), (2, 1, 2)\\]\" width=\"243\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/d/35d0bd0c558f6fef60c126f35e5b623b48b30a88.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(3, 3, 0), (3, 0, 3)\\]\" width=\"120\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/5/5e5fa81d27c7e81f7f8b7f5c1aa4698666d72137.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(4, 2, 0), (4, 0, 2)\\]\" width=\"120\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/8/2a82232c65141a6756f8465f1bdd4db9230c1f8d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(5, 1, 0), (5, 0, 1)\\]\" width=\"120\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/d/30da2506ed4e8b64d2db0f8157700eb1bfb2b7b2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(6, 0, 0)\\]\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>There are little tricks we can use to make the process of adding up the corresponding outcomes faster. First, we notice that the first, second, and last row of solutions contributes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/a/bdab99612d6ec89cecfa082adb0afdbf8ceee4ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^7=128$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"67\" height=\"15\" /> outcomes. Then we can take advantage of the symmetry between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/5/32581661cddf01ad30c1e36efe72f99eb0a0adca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" /> to add up the rest of the outcomes. In the end we get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/9/d09320b4596093e8da6b1fc0fc32febc98e99f7d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[128+2(6+180+15+180+20+15+6)=972\\]\" width=\"382\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Hence, the probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/f/71fceb3ae25c955705f3b43bf4deba632d94ef28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{972}{4^6}=\\frac{243}{2^{10}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"89\" height=\"38\" />. Therefore, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/8/f0813d5c579b9571a42071d904e723c8fc905f87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\fbox{\\textbf{(A) } 243}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"79\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~tsun26\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_Symmetry_.2B_Observations_.3D_5_min_solve\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_Symmetry_+_Observations_=_5_min_solve\">Solution 2 Symmetry + Observations = 5 min solve</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 Symmetry + Observations = 5 min solve\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be the number of balls with stripes, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> the number of red balls, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> the number of balls that are red and have stripes.\nNow, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/3/9f3d1fa93908642e58e8e64d220c7f8a499ee4ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(A\\ \\text{and}\\ B) = P(A) \\cdot P(B) \\Rightarrow \\frac{N}{6}=\\frac{S \\cdot R}{36}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"335\" height=\"39\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/4/4a409446d0ca5d8a28ba472c7ca8af1f96170cce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6N = SR$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"78\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Make the trivial observation that whenever <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, or both equal <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, there is one unique <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> satisfying the equality.\nNotice for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/4/7b4bdf3b9129bb9321e54025fc7078fe77032571.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S = 0$\" width=\"45\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> going from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we have a total probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/9/9c90d44eba9d56770167403fd69e39915a39c3db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^6}{2^{12}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"29\" height=\"41\" /> due to binomial theorem.\nBy symmetry, we must multiply by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to account for switching <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />, and another <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> for the case where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/6/926f91b9410779c4e5c6d74d30da3935ae8294b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S=R=6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"14\" />. Finally, we subtract the four overlapping cases <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/6/b663190b1b560fa362f97c0cf703a220ce5d0d47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 0), (0, 6), (6,0),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"144\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/d/0dd1499dbb1ea032542c13537a2ca2f157d4c832.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, where each pair has format <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/d/97d0453d2aa6a028475356705511a0bf0e8e0771.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(S, R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"49\" height=\"20\" />.\nThus, the total probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/f/1bfc7cf19b3f9a9e95c9daea2d56d6757694d017.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4 \\cdot 2^6 - 4}{2^{12}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"76\" height=\"41\" />.\n</p><p>Now, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> more pairs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/8/e787d9386e58a10dc41cb20f87af676add177bff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(S,R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"49\" height=\"20\" /> that satisfy the condition, namely <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/d/5bd4ada354acfb3f11a6ad65e20db040ce6a3525.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 3), (3, 4), (3, 2),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"144\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/4/7347741cade420c9172f1251847efddfdeb489ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4, 3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Again, make the trivial observation that each of these have the same probability due to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/c/b7ccefd9027bba4f9ad4b4860dd569a02b2c993f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R/S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"37\" height=\"20\" /> invariance and similar distribution. \n</p><p>For a single pair, take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/a/68a601c8e837eafe7d899c2ba96295b3637daf6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> as an example. We know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/b/ffbf0e3d6449ddf18d2352976ba403dc41dd46b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" />, and we fix <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. The probability is therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/2/5a2a76ce5af15fa911ea78d9fcebebdaaf993abc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\binom{6}{3} \\binom{3}{1} \\binom{3}{1}}{2^{12}} = \\frac{180}{2^{12}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"130\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p><p>Adding all of them up, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/0/910a3f6b35706dc85e65b71099df1fa16a629a60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4 \\cdot 2^6 - 4 + 180 \\cdot 4}{2^{12}} = \\frac{2^6+179}{2^{10}} = \\frac{243}{1024}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"306\" height=\"41\" />.\nThus, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/e/f6eb65962292d994ce2f9a8af63728883f256b18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"27\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>~Apollo Luo, edited by Mathemagician108\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://youtu.be/V9YKdrGLQdE?si=2W17enGPJ2NqQ6r3\">2024 AMC 12 B #25</a>\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://mathproblemsolvingskills.wordpress.com/\">MathProblemSolvingSkills.com</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_25&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-